/* ISC Biology (863) — Class XI & XII */
window.SM = window.SM || {subjects:{}};
window.SM.subjects.biology = {
  id:'biology', name:'Biology', code:'863', icon:'🧬', colour:'#2fa96b',
  pattern:'Paper I Theory 70 marks (3 hrs) · Paper II Practical 15 + Project 10 + File 5 = 30 marks',
  classes:{
  12:{ groups:[{marks:16,units:[1]},{marks:15,units:[2]},{marks:14,units:[3]},{marks:10,units:[4]},{marks:15,units:[5]}],
  chapters:[
  { id:'b12-1', unit:1, name:'Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants', marks:'Unit 1 — Reproduction (16)',
    topics:['Flower structure; development of male and female gametophytes','Pollination: types, agencies and examples; outbreeding devices','Pollen–pistil interaction; double fertilisation','Post-fertilisation events: development of endosperm and embryo, seed and fruit','Special modes: apomixis, parthenocarpy, polyembryony','Significance of seed and fruit formation'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Microsporogenesis', d:'Formation of microspores (pollen grains) from the microspore mother cell by meiosis inside the anther. The pollen grain has a two-layered wall: outer exine of sporopollenin (highly resistant) and inner intine of cellulose and pectin.'},
      {t:'Megasporogenesis', d:'Formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell by meiosis in the nucellus. Usually only the chalazal megaspore survives to form the embryo sac (monosporic development).'},
      {t:'Embryo sac', d:'The typical 7-celled, 8-nucleate female gametophyte: 3 antipodals, 2 synergids with the filiform apparatus, one egg cell and a large central cell with two polar nuclei.'},
      {t:'Double fertilisation', d:'One male gamete fuses with the egg to form the diploid zygote (syngamy); the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid primary endosperm nucleus (triple fusion). Unique to angiosperms.'},
      {t:'Outbreeding devices', d:'Devices preventing self-pollination: dichogamy (different maturation times), self-incompatibility, herkogamy (physical separation), unisexuality and male sterility.'},
      {t:'Apomixis', d:'Production of seed without fertilisation — a form of asexual reproduction mimicking sexual. Valuable in hybrid seed production because the hybrid vigour is retained year after year.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Anther wall (outside in): epidermis → endothecium → middle layers → tapetum', m:'Tapetum nourishes the developing pollen'},
      {f:'Ploidy: nucellus 2n · MMC 2n · embryo sac n · egg n · endosperm 3n · zygote 2n · perisperm 2n', m:'Frequently asked in exams'},
      {f:'Pollen grain at shedding: 2-celled (60%) or 3-celled (40%)', m:'Vegetative + generative cell'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying the endosperm is diploid — it is triploid (3n) in angiosperms.','Confusing the pericarp (fruit wall, from ovary wall) with the seed coat (from integuments).','Forgetting that the filiform apparatus in the synergid guides the pollen tube.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'What is double fertilisation? Why is it significant?', a:'In angiosperms two male gametes released by the pollen tube take part in fertilisation. One fuses with the egg cell forming a diploid zygote — this is syngamy. The other fuses with the two polar nuclei of the central cell forming the triploid primary endosperm nucleus — this is triple fusion. Since two fusions occur, the event is called double fertilisation. Its significance is that endosperm formation is linked to fertilisation, so nutritive tissue develops only when an embryo is actually formed, avoiding wastage of resources.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe the structure of a typical mature embryo sac.', a:'It is 7-celled and 8-nucleate. At the micropylar end lies the egg apparatus — one egg cell and two synergids bearing a filiform apparatus that guides the pollen tube. At the chalazal end lie three antipodal cells. In the centre is the large central cell containing two polar nuclei. All are haploid; the central cell effectively contributes two nuclei to triple fusion.'},
      {marks:2, q:'What is apomixis and what is its importance in agriculture?', a:'Apomixis is the formation of seeds without fertilisation, a type of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction. Its importance is that hybrid varieties, which normally lose their vigour when their seeds are sown because of segregation, would breed true if made apomictic. Farmers could then keep using their own seed year after year instead of buying fresh hybrid seed each season.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Name two outbreeding devices in plants and explain how each works.', a:'(i) Dichogamy — the anthers and stigma of the same flower mature at different times (protandry or protogyny), so self-pollination is prevented. (ii) Self-incompatibility — a genetic mechanism in which pollen from the same plant fails to germinate on the stigma or the pollen tube fails to reach the ovule. (Others: herkogamy, unisexual flowers, male sterility.)'},
      {marks:3, q:'Give the ploidy of: nucellus, megaspore mother cell, functional megaspore, antipodal cell, primary endosperm nucleus, and perisperm.', a:'Nucellus — diploid (2n). Megaspore mother cell — diploid (2n). Functional megaspore — haploid (n). Antipodal cell — haploid (n). Primary endosperm nucleus — triploid (3n). Perisperm — diploid (2n), as it is the persistent remnant of the nucellus.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-2', unit:1, name:'Human Reproduction', marks:'Unit 1 — Reproduction (16)',
    topics:['Male and female reproductive systems','Microscopic anatomy of testis and ovary','Gametogenesis: spermatogenesis and oogenesis','Menstrual cycle','Fertilisation, embryo development up to blastocyst, implantation','Pregnancy and placenta formation','Parturition and lactation'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Spermatogenesis', d:'Begins at puberty, continues throughout life. Spermatogonia (2n) → primary spermatocyte (2n) → two secondary spermatocytes (n) → four spermatids (n) → four sperms. One spermatogonium gives four functional sperms.'},
      {t:'Oogenesis', d:'Begins in foetal life; oogonia stop dividing and are arrested at prophase I. One oogonium gives only one ovum plus three polar bodies. Meiosis II completes only if fertilisation occurs.'},
      {t:'Menstrual cycle', d:'About 28 days, divided into menstrual (1–5), follicular/proliferative (5–14), ovulatory (14) and luteal/secretory (15–28) phases. A sharp LH surge causes ovulation.'},
      {t:'Placenta', d:'A structural and functional connection between foetus and mother, formed from chorionic villi and uterine tissue. It supplies nutrients and O₂, removes wastes and CO₂, and acts as an endocrine gland secreting hCG, hPL, oestrogen, progesterone and relaxin.'},
      {t:'Capacitation', d:'The physiological changes sperms must undergo in the female tract before they can fertilise the ovum.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Sperm structure: head (nucleus + acrosome) · neck (centrioles) · middle piece (mitochondria) · tail', m:'Acrosome has hyaluronidase; middle piece supplies energy'},
      {f:'Hormone sequence: FSH → follicle growth · LH surge → ovulation · progesterone → maintains endometrium', m:'Menstrual cycle control'},
      {f:'hCG, hPL, relaxin — secreted only in pregnancy', m:'Basis of pregnancy tests'},
      {f:'Zygote → morula (8–16 cells) → blastocyst → implantation (~7 days)', m:'Early development'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying oogenesis produces four ova — it produces one ovum and three polar bodies.','Forgetting that the secondary oocyte is released at ovulation, not a mature ovum.','Mixing up the sources of hormones: hCG comes from the trophoblast, not the ovary.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Compare spermatogenesis and oogenesis in three respects.', a:'(i) Timing: spermatogenesis begins at puberty and continues throughout life; oogenesis begins in foetal life, is arrested, and is completed only after puberty and up to menopause. (ii) Products: one spermatogonium gives four functional sperms; one oogonium gives one ovum and three non-functional polar bodies. (iii) Cytoplasmic division: equal in spermatogenesis, highly unequal in oogenesis so that the ovum retains almost all the cytoplasm and stored food.'},
      {marks:5, q:'Describe the menstrual cycle and the hormonal control of its phases.', a:'The cycle averages 28 days. Menstrual phase (days 1–5): the endometrium breaks down and is shed as the corpus luteum degenerates and progesterone falls. Follicular phase (days 5–13): FSH from the anterior pituitary stimulates growth of a Graafian follicle, which secretes oestrogen; oestrogen causes proliferation of the endometrium. Ovulation (day 14): rising oestrogen triggers a sharp LH surge which ruptures the follicle and releases the secondary oocyte. Luteal phase (days 15–28): LH converts the ruptured follicle into the corpus luteum, which secretes large amounts of progesterone to maintain the endometrium for implantation. If fertilisation does not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates, progesterone falls and menstruation begins again.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe the structure and functions of the placenta.', a:'The placenta is a disc-shaped structure formed by the interdigitation of chorionic villi of the embryo with the uterine tissue of the mother. It is connected to the foetus by the umbilical cord. Functions: (i) supplies oxygen and nutrients from the maternal blood to the foetus, (ii) removes carbon dioxide and nitrogenous wastes from the foetus, (iii) acts as an endocrine gland secreting hCG, human placental lactogen, oestrogen, progesterone and relaxin, and (iv) acts as a barrier preventing the mixing of maternal and foetal blood while allowing some antibodies through.'},
      {marks:2, q:'What is capacitation and where does it occur?', a:'Capacitation is the set of physiological changes that sperms must undergo in the female reproductive tract before they become capable of fertilising the ovum. The glycoprotein coat on the sperm head is removed, making the acrosome ready to release its enzymes. It takes place in the female genital tract, and takes about 5–6 hours in humans.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-3', unit:1, name:'Reproductive Health', marks:'Unit 1 — Reproduction (16)',
    topics:['Need for reproductive health; prevention of sexually transmitted infections','Birth control: need and methods; contraception and medical termination of pregnancy','Amniocentesis and its misuse','Infertility and assisted reproductive technologies: IVF, ZIFT, GIFT, ICSI, artificial insemination'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'RCH programme', d:'Reproductive and Child Health Care programme — aims at creating awareness, providing facilities and support for a reproductively healthy society.'},
      {t:'Contraceptive categories', d:'Natural (periodic abstinence, withdrawal, lactational amenorrhoea), barrier (condoms, diaphragm), IUDs (copper releasing, hormone releasing), oral pills, injectables/implants, and surgical (vasectomy, tubectomy).'},
      {t:'IVF vs GIFT vs ZIFT', d:'IVF: fertilisation outside the body, embryo transferred. ZIFT: zygote or early embryo (up to 8 blastomeres) transferred to the fallopian tube. GIFT: an ovum from a donor is transferred to the fallopian tube of a woman who cannot produce ova. ICSI: a single sperm is injected directly into the ovum.'},
      {t:'Amniocentesis', d:'A foetal sex and disorder determination test based on the chromosome pattern in the amniotic fluid. Legally banned for sex determination in India to prevent female foeticide.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'MTP — legally permitted in India up to 20 weeks (extended to 24 weeks for special categories)', m:'Medical termination of pregnancy'},
      {f:'Saheli — a non-steroidal oral contraceptive pill developed in India, taken once a week', m:'Developed at CDRI Lucknow'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing ZIFT (zygote) with GIFT (gamete).','Saying condoms only prevent pregnancy — they also protect against STIs and AIDS.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Differentiate between IVF, ZIFT and GIFT.', a:'IVF (in vitro fertilisation): ova and sperms are collected and fertilised in the laboratory; the resulting embryo is transferred either to the fallopian tube (ZIFT if up to 8 blastomeres) or to the uterus (IUT if beyond 8 blastomeres). ZIFT (zygote intra-fallopian transfer): the zygote or early embryo formed in vitro is placed in the fallopian tube. GIFT (gamete intra-fallopian transfer): an ovum collected from a donor is transferred into the fallopian tube of a woman who cannot produce ova but can provide a suitable environment for fertilisation and development.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Why is amniocentesis banned for sex determination in India?', a:'Although it is a valuable test for detecting chromosomal abnormalities and metabolic disorders in the foetus, it was widely misused to determine the sex of the foetus and abort female foetuses. This caused a serious fall in the child sex ratio. The Government of India has therefore legally banned its use for sex determination.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Explain how oral contraceptive pills prevent pregnancy.', a:'Oral pills contain progestogens either alone or with oestrogen. They act mainly by inhibiting ovulation — they suppress the release of FSH and LH from the pituitary so no follicle matures and no LH surge occurs. They also make the cervical mucus thick, hindering the entry of sperms, and alter the endometrium so that implantation is discouraged. They must be taken daily for 21 days starting within the first five days of the cycle.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-4', unit:2, name:'Principles of Inheritance and Variation', marks:'Unit 2 — Genetics and Evolution (15)',
    topics:['Mendel\'s laws of inheritance; monohybrid and dihybrid crosses','Deviations from Mendelism: incomplete dominance, co-dominance, multiple alleles, pleiotropy','Inheritance of blood groups','Polygenic inheritance','Chromosome theory of inheritance; linkage and crossing over','Sex determination in humans, birds and honey bee','Mendelian disorders: haemophilia, sickle cell anaemia, phenylketonuria, thalassemia','Chromosomal disorders: Down\'s, Turner\'s and Klinefelter\'s syndromes'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Law of segregation', d:'The two alleles of a pair separate during gamete formation so that each gamete receives only one. Universally valid — it has no exceptions.'},
      {t:'Law of independent assortment', d:'When two pairs of traits are considered, the segregation of one pair is independent of the other. Valid only for genes on different chromosomes or far apart on the same chromosome.'},
      {t:'Incomplete dominance vs codominance', d:'Incomplete dominance: the F₁ is intermediate (pink Antirrhinum), phenotypic ratio 1:2:1. Codominance: both alleles express fully and independently (AB blood group).'},
      {t:'Linkage and recombination', d:'Genes on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together. The frequency of recombination between two genes is proportional to the distance between them — the basis of genetic mapping.'},
      {t:'Pleiotropy', d:'A single gene affecting several apparently unrelated phenotypic traits, e.g. the gene for phenylketonuria affects mental development, hair and skin pigmentation.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Monohybrid F₂ — genotypic 1:2:1, phenotypic 3:1', m:'Mendel'},
      {f:'Dihybrid F₂ — phenotypic 9:3:3:1', m:'Independent assortment'},
      {f:'Test cross — cross with the double recessive to determine genotype', m:'F₁ × recessive parent'},
      {f:'Blood groups: I^A, I^B codominant; i recessive → 6 genotypes, 4 phenotypes', m:'Multiple alleles'},
      {f:'Down\'s = trisomy 21 (47) · Turner\'s = XO (45) · Klinefelter\'s = XXY (47)', m:'Chromosomal disorders'},
      {f:'Haemophilia and colour blindness — X-linked recessive', m:'Criss-cross inheritance'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Writing the dihybrid ratio as 9:3:3:1 for linked genes — linkage distorts it.','Confusing Turner\'s (XO, female) with Klinefelter\'s (XXY, male).','Saying haemophilia is more common in females — it is far more common in males.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'State Mendel\'s law of independent assortment. Why does it not hold for linked genes?', a:'When two pairs of contrasting characters are considered together, the assortment of one pair is independent of the other. It holds because the pairs of homologous chromosomes align independently at metaphase I. Linked genes lie on the same chromosome and therefore move together into the same gamete, so parental combinations appear far more often than recombinants and the expected 9:3:3:1 ratio is not obtained.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the inheritance of ABO blood groups in humans.', a:'The trait is controlled by a gene I with three alleles — I^A, I^B and i — an example of multiple allelism. I^A and I^B are codominant with each other and both are dominant over i. This gives six genotypes and four phenotypes: I^AI^A or I^Ai = group A; I^BI^B or I^Bi = group B; I^AI^B = group AB (both antigens expressed, showing codominance); ii = group O. It is also an example of a single gene showing more than two alleles in the population, though any one individual carries only two.'},
      {marks:3, q:'A colour blind man marries a woman with normal vision whose father was colour blind. Work out the probability of their children being colour blind.', a:'Colour blindness is X-linked recessive. Father: X^cY (colour blind). Mother: her father was X^cY so she must have received X^c from him — she is a carrier, X^X^c. Cross X^cY × X^X^c gives X^X^c (carrier daughter), X^cX^c (colour blind daughter), X^Y (normal son), X^cY (colour blind son). So 50% of the children are colour blind — half the daughters and half the sons.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Name the chromosomal abnormality and karyotype in (i) Down\'s syndrome, (ii) Klinefelter\'s syndrome.', a:'(i) Down\'s syndrome — trisomy of chromosome 21, karyotype 47, with three copies of chromosome 21. Caused by non-disjunction during gamete formation; risk rises with maternal age. (ii) Klinefelter\'s syndrome — an additional X chromosome, karyotype 47, XXY. The individual is male but sterile with some feminine development.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What is a test cross? Why is it useful?', a:'A test cross is a cross between an individual showing the dominant phenotype but of unknown genotype and the homozygous recessive parent. If the offspring are all dominant, the unknown parent is homozygous dominant (TT); if the offspring appear in a 1:1 ratio of dominant to recessive, the unknown parent is heterozygous (Tt). It is useful because homozygous and heterozygous dominants look identical, and a test cross is the only way to distinguish them from the phenotype of the offspring.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-5', unit:2, name:'Molecular Basis of Inheritance', marks:'Unit 2 — Genetics and Evolution (15)',
    topics:['Search for genetic material; DNA as genetic material; structure of DNA and RNA','DNA packaging; nucleosome','DNA replication','Central dogma; transcription; genetic code; translation','Gene expression and regulation — lac operon','Genome, Human Genome Project and DNA fingerprinting'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Griffith\'s experiment', d:'Showed transformation — heat-killed virulent S strain could transform live non-virulent R strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae into the virulent form. Avery, MacLeod and McCarty later showed the transforming principle was DNA.'},
      {t:'Hershey–Chase experiment', d:'Using bacteriophages labelled with ³²P (DNA) and ³⁵S (protein), they showed only ³²P entered the bacterial cell — conclusive proof that DNA, not protein, is the genetic material.'},
      {t:'Semi-conservative replication', d:'Each daughter DNA molecule has one parental and one newly synthesised strand. Proved by Meselson and Stahl using ¹⁵N labelling of E. coli.'},
      {t:'Genetic code features', d:'Triplet, universal, degenerate (more than one codon per amino acid), non-overlapping, comma-less, and unambiguous. AUG is the initiator and codes for methionine; UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons.'},
      {t:'lac operon', d:'An inducible operon: in the absence of lactose the repressor binds the operator and blocks transcription; lactose (the inducer) binds the repressor and inactivates it, allowing transcription of z, y and a genes for β-galactosidase, permease and transacetylase.'},
      {t:'DNA fingerprinting', d:'Based on variable number tandem repeats (VNTRs) — satellite DNA that differs between individuals. Used in forensics, paternity testing and studying population diversity.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Chargaff\'s rule: A = T and G = C ; (A+G) = (T+C)', m:'Base equivalence'},
      {f:'B-DNA: 2 nm wide, pitch 3.4 nm, 10 base pairs per turn, rise 0.34 nm per bp', m:'Watson–Crick model'},
      {f:'Nucleosome = 200 bp of DNA + histone octamer (2 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4)', m:'Packaging'},
      {f:'Central dogma: DNA →transcription→ RNA →translation→ Protein', m:'Reverse transcription in retroviruses'},
      {f:'Human genome: ~3.1 billion bp, ~20,500 genes', m:'Human Genome Project (1990–2003)'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying replication is conservative — it is semi-conservative.','Writing that RNA polymerase needs a primer (DNA polymerase does; RNA polymerase does not).','Confusing the lac operon (inducible, catabolic) with a repressible operon.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:5, q:'Describe the Hershey–Chase experiment and state its conclusion.', a:'Hershey and Chase worked with bacteriophage T2 infecting E. coli. They grew phages in a medium containing radioactive phosphorus (³²P), which labels DNA only, and separately in radioactive sulphur (³⁵S), which labels protein only. They allowed each set to infect bacteria, then agitated the culture in a blender to dislodge the viral coats from the bacterial surface and centrifuged it. Bacteria infected with ³²P-labelled phages were radioactive, showing DNA had entered the cell; those infected with ³⁵S-labelled phages were not, showing the protein coat had stayed outside. The conclusion is that DNA, and not protein, is the genetic material passed from virus to host.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the lac operon and its regulation.', a:'The lac operon of E. coli consists of a regulator gene (i), a promoter (p), an operator (o) and three structural genes — z (β-galactosidase), y (permease) and a (transacetylase). In the absence of lactose, the i gene produces a repressor protein which binds the operator and prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing the structural genes. When lactose is available it acts as an inducer: it binds the repressor and changes its shape so it can no longer bind the operator. RNA polymerase then transcribes the structural genes and the enzymes for lactose metabolism are produced. This is negative regulation of an inducible operon.'},
      {marks:3, q:'List five salient features of the genetic code.', a:'(i) It is a triplet code — three nucleotides specify one amino acid, giving 64 codons. (ii) It is degenerate — most amino acids are coded by more than one codon. (iii) It is non-overlapping and comma-less — read continuously, three bases at a time. (iv) It is unambiguous — one codon specifies only one amino acid. (v) It is nearly universal — the same codons mean the same amino acids from bacteria to humans. AUG serves both as the initiator codon and as the codon for methionine, and UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What is DNA fingerprinting? Give two applications.', a:'DNA fingerprinting is a technique for identifying differences in specific regions of DNA — chiefly the repetitive satellite DNA called VNTRs, whose copy number varies greatly between individuals. DNA is isolated, digested with restriction enzymes, separated by electrophoresis, blotted onto a membrane (Southern blotting), hybridised with a labelled VNTR probe and detected by autoradiography, giving a band pattern unique to each individual except identical twins. Applications: (i) forensic identification of criminals from blood, semen or hair at a crime scene; (ii) settling paternity and maternity disputes. It is also used in studying genetic diversity in populations.'},
      {marks:2, q:'State Chargaff\'s rules and their significance.', a:'In any double stranded DNA the amount of adenine equals that of thymine and the amount of guanine equals that of cytosine, so (A + G) = (T + C). Their significance is that they pointed directly to specific base pairing between purines and pyrimidines, and were a key clue used by Watson and Crick in building the double helix model.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-6', unit:2, name:'Evolution', marks:'Unit 2 — Genetics and Evolution (15)',
    topics:['Origin of life; theories of chemical evolution; Miller–Urey experiment','Evidences for evolution: palaeontological, comparative anatomy, embryology and molecular','Darwin\'s theory of natural selection; example of industrial melanism','Modern synthetic theory; Hardy–Weinberg principle','Types of natural selection; adaptive radiation','Human evolution'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Homologous vs analogous organs', d:'Homologous: same basic structure and origin, different functions — divergent evolution (forelimbs of whale, bat, human). Analogous: different structure and origin, same function — convergent evolution (wings of butterfly and bird).'},
      {t:'Hardy–Weinberg principle', d:'In a large, randomly mating population with no mutation, migration, genetic drift or selection, allele frequencies remain constant across generations. Any deviation indicates evolution is occurring.'},
      {t:'Genetic drift', d:'Random change in allele frequency, significant only in small populations. Includes the founder effect and the bottleneck effect.'},
      {t:'Adaptive radiation', d:'Evolution of different species from a common ancestor in a geographical area, each adapted to a different niche — Darwin\'s finches on the Galapagos, Australian marsupials.'},
      {t:'Industrial melanism', d:'In pre-industrial England the white-winged peppered moth was common; after industrialisation soot darkened the tree trunks and the dark-winged form became common — a direct observation of natural selection.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'p + q = 1 ; p² + 2pq + q² = 1', m:'Hardy–Weinberg equation'},
      {f:'Five factors that disturb equilibrium: gene migration, genetic drift, mutation, genetic recombination, natural selection', m:'Causes of evolution'},
      {f:'Human line: Dryopithecus → Ramapithecus → Australopithecus → Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Homo neanderthalensis → Homo sapiens', m:'Human evolution sequence'},
      {f:'Miller–Urey: CH₄, NH₃, H₂, water vapour + electric discharge (800°C) → amino acids', m:'Chemical evolution'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing homologous with analogous organs.','Saying Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium proves evolution — its disturbance does.','Attributing acquired characters to Darwin — that was Lamarck.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'State the Hardy–Weinberg principle and write its equation. What does a deviation indicate?', a:'In a large randomly mating population, the allele frequencies and genotype frequencies remain constant from generation to generation, provided there is no mutation, no gene migration, no genetic drift and no natural selection. If p and q are the frequencies of two alleles, p + q = 1 and the genotype frequencies are given by p² + 2pq + q² = 1. A measured deviation from these expected values means one or more of the five factors is at work, i.e. the population is evolving.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Distinguish between homologous and analogous organs with examples. What type of evolution does each indicate?', a:'Homologous organs have the same fundamental structure and embryonic origin but perform different functions — the forelimbs of a whale (swimming), a bat (flying), a cheetah (running) and a human (grasping) all have the same bone plan. They indicate divergent evolution from a common ancestor. Analogous organs have different structure and origin but perform the same function — the wings of a butterfly and of a bird, or the eyes of an octopus and a mammal. They indicate convergent evolution, where unrelated groups develop similar adaptations under similar selection pressures.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe the Miller–Urey experiment and its significance.', a:'In 1953 S. L. Miller created conditions believed to resemble the primitive earth in a closed flask: methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapour, with electric discharges from electrodes at 800°C simulating lightning, and a condenser to return the water. After a week he found amino acids and other simple organic molecules in the flask. Its significance is that it gave the first experimental support for the theory of chemical evolution — that the first organic molecules could have arisen abiotically from simple inorganic gases under primitive earth conditions.'},
      {marks:2, q:'What is adaptive radiation? Give one example.', a:'Adaptive radiation is the evolution of a variety of different species from a single ancestral form, each adapted to a different habitat or way of life, when the ancestor colonises a new area with many available niches. Darwin\'s finches on the Galapagos islands are the classic example — from one seed-eating ancestor arose many varieties with beaks specialised for insects, cactus, seeds and so on.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-7', unit:3, name:'Human Health and Diseases', marks:'Unit 3 — Biology and Human Welfare (14)',
    topics:['Pathogens and parasites causing human diseases: malaria, filariasis, ascariasis, typhoid, pneumonia, common cold, amoebiasis, ringworm','Basic concepts of immunology: innate and acquired immunity, active and passive immunity','Vaccines and immunisation','Allergies; auto-immunity; the immune system in the body','AIDS and cancer','Adolescence, drug and alcohol abuse'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Innate vs acquired immunity', d:'Innate is non-specific and present from birth — physical, physiological, cellular and cytokine barriers. Acquired is pathogen specific and has memory — mediated by B lymphocytes (humoral, antibodies) and T lymphocytes (cell mediated).'},
      {t:'Active vs passive immunity', d:'Active: the body makes its own antibodies after exposure to an antigen (vaccination, infection); slow to develop but long lasting. Passive: ready-made antibodies are given (anti-tetanus serum, mother\'s colostrum); immediate but short lived.'},
      {t:'Antibody structure', d:'H₂L₂ — two heavy and two light polypeptide chains. Five types: IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG, IgM. IgG crosses the placenta; IgA is present in colostrum.'},
      {t:'AIDS', d:'Caused by HIV, a retrovirus. It infects helper T cells, uses reverse transcriptase to make viral DNA that integrates into the host genome. The helper T count falls, so immunity collapses and the patient dies of secondary infections. Diagnosed by ELISA.'},
      {t:'Cancer', d:'Uncontrolled cell division due to loss of contact inhibition, producing a tumour. Benign tumours remain localised; malignant ones invade and metastasise. Caused by carcinogens — physical, chemical or biological — acting on proto-oncogenes.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Malaria — Plasmodium, female Anopheles vector, haemozoin causes chill and fever every 3–4 days', m:'Sporozoite is the infective stage'},
      {f:'Typhoid — Salmonella typhi ; test: Widal', m:'Small intestine'},
      {f:'Amoebiasis — Entamoeba histolytica ; vector: housefly', m:'Amoebic dysentery'},
      {f:'Filariasis — Wuchereria ; Ascariasis — Ascaris ; Ringworm — Microsporum, Trichophyton, Epidermophyton', m:'Other pathogens'},
      {f:'Cancer detection: biopsy, histopathology, CT, MRI, monoclonal antibodies', m:'Treatment: surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy (α-interferon)'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying HIV directly kills the patient — death is from secondary infections.','Confusing active immunity (slow, lasting) with passive (fast, temporary).','Naming the male Anopheles as the malaria vector — it is the female.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Differentiate between active and passive immunity with one example each.', a:'Active immunity develops when the body is exposed to an antigen — through infection or vaccination — and produces its own antibodies and memory cells. It takes time to develop but lasts long. Example: immunity following polio vaccination. Passive immunity is conferred by giving ready-made antibodies from outside. It acts immediately but is short lived and gives no memory. Example: anti-tetanus serum given after injury, or the antibodies a baby receives in the mother\'s colostrum.'},
      {marks:5, q:'Describe the replication of HIV inside the human body and the resulting symptoms.', a:'HIV enters the body through infected blood, sexual contact or from mother to child. It infects macrophages, where the viral RNA is copied into DNA by reverse transcriptase. This viral DNA integrates into the host cell DNA and directs the production of new viruses. The macrophage acts as an HIV factory. The virus then enters helper T lymphocytes, replicates in them and destroys them. Their numbers fall progressively over years, so the immune system becomes unable to fight infection. The patient suffers from bouts of fever, diarrhoea and weight loss, and dies of opportunistic infections such as tuberculosis, fungal infections and certain cancers. Diagnosis is by ELISA; treatment with anti-retroviral drugs prolongs life but does not cure.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What is metastasis? How does a malignant tumour differ from a benign one?', a:'Metastasis is the property by which cells sloughed off from a malignant tumour reach distant sites through the blood and start new tumours there. A benign tumour remains confined to its original site, grows slowly and does not spread, causing limited damage. A malignant tumour grows rapidly, invades and damages surrounding normal tissue, competes with normal cells for nutrients, and spreads by metastasis — which is what makes it life threatening.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Name the causative organism and one symptom each of malaria and typhoid.', a:'Malaria — caused by Plasmodium (P. vivax, P. malariae, P. falciparum), transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito; symptom: recurring chill and high fever every three to four days, caused by the toxin haemozoin released when red cells rupture. Typhoid — caused by Salmonella typhi, transmitted through contaminated food and water; symptoms: sustained high fever (39–40°C), weakness, stomach pain and constipation, confirmed by the Widal test.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-8', unit:3, name:'Microbes in Human Welfare', marks:'Unit 3 — Biology and Human Welfare (14)',
    topics:['Microbes in household products','Microbes in industrial products: fermented beverages, antibiotics, chemicals, enzymes and bioactive molecules','Microbes in sewage treatment: primary and secondary treatment','Microbes in production of biogas','Microbes as biocontrol agents and as biofertilisers'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Sewage treatment', d:'Primary treatment is physical removal of solids by filtration and sedimentation. Secondary (biological) treatment uses aerobic microbes in aeration tanks to form flocs and reduce BOD, followed by anaerobic digestion of the sludge which produces biogas.'},
      {t:'BOD', d:'Biochemical oxygen demand — the amount of oxygen consumed by bacteria to oxidise the organic matter in one litre of water. Higher BOD means more polluting.'},
      {t:'Biofertilisers', d:'Organisms that enrich soil nutrients — Rhizobium (symbiotic N₂ fixation in legume root nodules), Azotobacter (free living), Anabaena and Nostoc (cyanobacteria in paddy fields), and Glomus (mycorrhiza, absorbs phosphorus).'},
      {t:'Biocontrol agents', d:'Bacillus thuringiensis for caterpillars, Trichoderma for plant root pathogens, Baculovirus (Nucleopolyhedrovirus) which is species specific, and ladybird and dragonfly for aphids and mosquitoes.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Curd — Lactobacillus ; Bread — Saccharomyces cerevisiae ; Toddy — from sap of palms', m:'Household products'},
      {f:'Penicillin — Penicillium notatum (Fleming, Chain, Florey — Nobel 1945)', m:'First antibiotic'},
      {f:'Citric acid — Aspergillus niger ; Acetic acid — Acetobacter aceti ; Butyric acid — Clostridium butylicum', m:'Industrial chemicals'},
      {f:'Statins — Monascus purpureus (blood cholesterol lowering) ; Cyclosporin A — Trichoderma polysporum (immunosuppressant)', m:'Bioactive molecules'},
      {f:'Biogas — methanogens such as Methanobacterium ; 50–70% methane', m:'Gobar gas plant'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing which microbe makes which product — this is heavily tested.','Saying primary treatment is biological — it is physical.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:5, q:'Describe primary and secondary sewage treatment.', a:'Primary treatment is a physical process. The sewage is passed through filters to remove floating debris, then allowed to settle so that grit and soil sink out. The settled solids form the primary sludge and the supernatant is the effluent. Secondary treatment is biological. The effluent is passed into large aeration tanks where it is constantly agitated and air is pumped in. Aerobic microbes grow rapidly and form flocs — masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments. They consume the organic matter, sharply reducing the BOD. The effluent is then passed to a settling tank where the flocs sediment as activated sludge. A small part of this is used as inoculum; the rest goes into anaerobic sludge digesters, where anaerobic bacteria digest it and release biogas. The treated effluent is finally released into natural water bodies.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What are biofertilisers? Describe three types with examples.', a:'Biofertilisers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil naturally. (i) Symbiotic nitrogen fixers — Rhizobium lives in the root nodules of leguminous plants and fixes atmospheric nitrogen into a form the plant can use. (ii) Free living nitrogen fixers — Azotobacter and Azospirillum live free in the soil and fix nitrogen; cyanobacteria such as Anabaena and Nostoc do the same in paddy fields. (iii) Mycorrhiza — the fungus Glomus forms a symbiotic association with plant roots and greatly increases the absorption of phosphorus, besides giving resistance to root pathogens and drought.'},
      {marks:2, q:'How is Bacillus thuringiensis used as a biocontrol agent?', a:'Dried spores of Bacillus thuringiensis are mixed with water and sprayed on plants. When a caterpillar eats the sprayed leaves, the alkaline gut dissolves the crystals and releases the Bt toxin, which perforates the gut epithelium and kills the insect. It is valuable because it is specific to certain insect groups and harmless to other insects, plants and mammals, unlike broad-spectrum chemical insecticides.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-9', unit:4, name:'Biotechnology: Principles, Processes and Applications', marks:'Unit 4 (10)',
    topics:['Principles of biotechnology: genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering','Tools of recombinant DNA technology: restriction enzymes, vectors, host organism','Processes of recombinant DNA technology; PCR; gel electrophoresis','Applications in health: genetically engineered insulin, gene therapy, molecular diagnosis','Applications in agriculture: Bt cotton, pest resistant plants, RNA interference','Transgenic animals; ethical issues, biopiracy and patents'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Restriction enzymes', d:'Molecular scissors that cut DNA at specific palindromic sequences, producing sticky ends. EcoRI, from Escherichia coli RY13, cuts at GAATTC. Sticky ends allow DNA from different sources to be joined by DNA ligase.'},
      {t:'Vector requirements', d:'A cloning vector needs an origin of replication (ori), a selectable marker, and a recognition site for restriction enzymes. Examples: plasmids (pBR322), bacteriophages, Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens.'},
      {t:'PCR', d:'Polymerase chain reaction amplifies a DNA segment in vitro through repeated cycles of denaturation (94°C), annealing of primers (~55°C) and extension by the thermostable Taq polymerase (72°C). A billion copies in about 30 cycles.'},
      {t:'Bt cotton', d:'Carries cry genes from Bacillus thuringiensis. The protein exists as inactive protoxin crystals; in the alkaline gut of the insect it is solubilised and activated, creating pores in the gut wall and killing the pest. cryIAc and cryIIAb control bollworms; cryIAb controls corn borer.'},
      {t:'RNA interference', d:'Silencing of a specific mRNA by a complementary double stranded RNA, which prevents translation. Used to make tobacco plants resistant to the nematode Meloidogyne incognita.'},
      {t:'Gene therapy', d:'Correction of a genetic defect by introducing a normal gene. The first clinical case (1990) treated a girl with ADA deficiency; lymphocytes carrying a functional ADA cDNA were returned to her blood, but as these are not immortal the infusion must be repeated.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'EcoRI cuts G↓AATTC — a palindromic sequence', m:'Sticky ends'},
      {f:'Steps: isolate DNA → cut with restriction enzyme → amplify by PCR → ligate into vector → transfer into host → select transformants → culture in bioreactor → downstream processing', m:'rDNA technology outline'},
      {f:'Eli Lilly insulin: A chain (21 aa) and B chain (30 aa) made separately in E. coli, then joined by disulphide bonds', m:'Humulin, 1983'},
      {f:'Rosie — first transgenic cow, produced human protein-enriched milk (2.4 g/L α-lactalbumin)', m:'Transgenic animals'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying the Bt toxin kills the bacterium — it exists as an inactive protoxin inside Bacillus.','Confusing biopiracy with bioprospecting.','Forgetting that PCR uses a thermostable polymerase from Thermus aquaticus.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:5, q:'Describe the steps involved in recombinant DNA technology.', a:'(i) Isolation of genetic material — cells are treated with lysozyme, cellulase or chitinase to break the wall, RNase and protease remove RNA and protein, and DNA is precipitated with chilled ethanol. (ii) Cutting DNA at specific sites — the DNA and the vector are both cut with the same restriction endonuclease so that complementary sticky ends are produced; the fragments are separated and identified by gel electrophoresis. (iii) Amplification — the gene of interest is amplified by PCR. (iv) Ligation — the gene and the cut vector are joined by DNA ligase to form recombinant DNA. (v) Insertion into the host — by heat shock with divalent calcium, microinjection, gene gun or a disarmed pathogen vector. (vi) Selection — transformants are identified using selectable markers or insertional inactivation. (vii) Culture in a bioreactor and downstream processing — separation, purification and formulation of the product.'},
      {marks:3, q:'How is genetically engineered insulin produced? What problem did it solve?', a:'Human insulin consists of an A chain of 21 amino acids and a B chain of 30 joined by disulphide bridges; in the body it is made as a single pro-insulin with an extra C peptide that must be removed. In 1983 Eli Lilly prepared the two DNA sequences corresponding to the A and B chains and introduced them into plasmids of E. coli, which produced the two chains separately. These were then extracted and combined by creating disulphide bonds to give human insulin (Humulin). Earlier, insulin was extracted from the pancreas of slaughtered cattle and pigs; it differed slightly from human insulin and caused allergic reactions in some patients. The engineered product is identical to human insulin and is available in unlimited quantity.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What is Bt cotton? Explain how the Bt toxin kills the insect but not the bacterium.', a:'Bt cotton is a transgenic cotton carrying cry genes from Bacillus thuringiensis, which make it resistant to bollworms. Inside the bacterium the toxin exists as an inactive protoxin in the form of crystals, so it does not harm the bacterium itself. When an insect eats the plant, the alkaline pH of its gut solubilises the crystal and converts the protoxin into the active toxin. This binds to the surface of the midgut epithelial cells, creates pores, and causes the cells to swell and lyse, killing the insect.'},
      {marks:2, q:'What is biopiracy? Give one example.', a:'Biopiracy is the use of bio-resources or traditional knowledge of a country by multinational companies and other organisations without proper authorisation from that country and without compensating its people. An example is the US patent granted for the healing properties of turmeric and for basmati rice — knowledge and varieties long used in India — both of which India successfully challenged.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-10', unit:5, name:'Organisms and Populations', marks:'Unit 5 — Ecology (15)',
    topics:['Organism and its environment; major abiotic factors; responses to abiotic factors','Adaptations','Population interactions: mutualism, competition, predation, parasitism','Population attributes: growth, birth rate and death rate, age distribution','Population growth models: exponential and logistic'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Regulate, conform, migrate, suspend', d:'Four ways organisms cope with stressful conditions. Regulators keep homeostasis (birds, mammals); conformers change with the environment (most animals and plants); some migrate; some suspend activity (hibernation, aestivation, diapause, seed dormancy).'},
      {t:'Allen\'s rule', d:'Mammals of colder climates have shorter ears and limbs to minimise heat loss.'},
      {t:'Gause\'s competitive exclusion principle', d:'Two closely related species competing for the same limiting resource cannot coexist indefinitely; the inferior competitor is eliminated.'},
      {t:'Exponential vs logistic growth', d:'Exponential (J-shaped) occurs when resources are unlimited. Logistic (S-shaped) occurs when resources are limited, levelling off at the carrying capacity K. Logistic is considered more realistic.'},
      {t:'Types of interaction', d:'Mutualism (+/+), competition (−/−), predation (+/−), parasitism (+/−), commensalism (+/0), amensalism (−/0).'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'dN/dt = rN → N_t = N₀e^{rt}', m:'Exponential growth; r is the intrinsic rate of natural increase'},
      {f:'dN/dt = rN(K − N)/K', m:'Logistic growth; K is the carrying capacity'},
      {f:'r = b − d', m:'Intrinsic rate of natural increase'},
      {f:'Ophrys and bee — sexual deceit ; Fig and wasp — obligate mutualism ; Mycorrhiza and Rhizobium — mutualism', m:'Classic examples'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing commensalism (one benefits, other unaffected) with mutualism (both benefit).','Writing the logistic equation without the (K − N)/K term.','Saying predators are always harmful — they keep prey populations in check and maintain species diversity.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Distinguish between exponential and logistic growth, giving their equations.', a:'Exponential growth occurs when food and space are unlimited; each species realises its full innate potential and the population grows without check. dN/dt = rN, which integrates to N_t = N₀e^{rt}, giving a J-shaped curve. Logistic growth occurs when resources are limited. Growth is slow at first (lag phase), then accelerates, then slows as competition increases and finally levels off at the carrying capacity K. dN/dt = rN(K − N)/K, giving a sigmoid or S-shaped curve. Since no habitat has unlimited resources, the logistic model is considered more realistic.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Explain, with examples, mutualism, commensalism and parasitism.', a:'Mutualism (+/+): both species benefit — the fig and its pollinator wasp, where the wasp pollinates the fig and uses some seeds for its larvae; also lichen and mycorrhiza. Commensalism (+/0): one benefits and the other is unaffected — an orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch, or barnacles on a whale. Parasitism (+/−): the parasite benefits at the host\'s cost — the human liver fluke, or Cuscuta on a hedge plant, which has lost its chlorophyll and draws nutrition from the host.'},
      {marks:2, q:'State Gause\'s competitive exclusion principle. Give one exception.', a:'Two closely related species competing for the same limiting resource cannot coexist indefinitely; the competitively inferior one will eventually be eliminated. However, species often coexist through resource partitioning — MacArthur showed five warbler species living on the same tree avoided competition by feeding in different zones of the canopy.'},
      {marks:2, q:'What is meant by adaptation? Give two examples of adaptation to desert conditions.', a:'An adaptation is any attribute of an organism — morphological, physiological or behavioural — that enables it to survive and reproduce in its habitat. Desert examples: (i) the kangaroo rat meets all its water needs by internal oxidation of fat and excretes highly concentrated urine; (ii) desert plants have a thick cuticle, sunken stomata, and follow the CAM pathway, keeping stomata closed by day to reduce transpiration — Opuntia has no leaves at all, the flattened stem carrying out photosynthesis.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-11', unit:5, name:'Ecosystem', marks:'Unit 5 — Ecology (15)',
    topics:['Ecosystem: patterns, components; productivity and decomposition','Energy flow; food chains and food webs','Ecological pyramids: number, biomass and energy','Ecological succession','Nutrient cycles: carbon and phosphorus','Ecosystem services'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Decomposition steps', d:'Fragmentation → leaching → catabolism → humification → mineralisation. Warm and moist conditions with nitrogen-rich, lignin-poor detritus decompose fastest.'},
      {t:'Ten percent law', d:'Only about 10% of the energy at one trophic level is transferred to the next; the rest is lost as heat in respiration. This limits food chains to 3–4 trophic levels.'},
      {t:'Ecological pyramids', d:'Pyramid of energy is always upright. Pyramids of number and biomass may be inverted — a single large tree supports many insects (inverted number), and in the sea a small standing crop of phytoplankton supports a large biomass of zooplankton and fish (inverted biomass).'},
      {t:'Succession', d:'The orderly change in the species composition of a community over time until a stable climax is reached. Primary succession starts on bare, lifeless areas (rock, new pond) and is very slow; secondary succession starts where a community was destroyed but soil remains, and is much faster.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'GPP − R = NPP', m:'Gross primary productivity minus respiration'},
      {f:'Annual net primary productivity of the biosphere ≈ 170 billion tonnes dry weight; oceans contribute only 55 billion despite covering 70%', m:'Productivity'},
      {f:'Energy flow: unidirectional, 10% law, food chains limited to 3–4 levels', m:'Trophic structure'},
      {f:'Grazing food chain (GFC) starts with producers; detritus food chain (DFC) starts with dead organic matter', m:'Two types of food chain'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying the pyramid of energy can be inverted — it never is.','Confusing GPP with NPP.','Assuming an organism belongs to only one trophic level — many occupy more than one.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the ten percent law with reference to energy flow in an ecosystem.', a:'Energy flow in an ecosystem is unidirectional, from the sun to producers to consumers. When energy passes from one trophic level to the next, only about 10% is stored as biomass in the next level; the remaining 90% is lost as heat during respiration and in metabolic activities, or is not consumed at all. This progressive loss is why food chains rarely have more than four or five trophic levels, and why the number of top carnivores an ecosystem can support is small.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Why is the pyramid of energy always upright while the pyramid of biomass may be inverted?', a:'The pyramid of energy is always upright because energy is lost at each transfer, so the energy content of any trophic level is always less than that of the level below — this follows from the laws of thermodynamics. The pyramid of biomass can be inverted because it measures the standing crop at a given moment, not the rate of production. In a sea, the phytoplankton have a very small standing biomass at any instant but reproduce extremely fast, so they can support a much larger biomass of zooplankton and fish above them, giving an inverted pyramid.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe primary and secondary succession and explain which is faster.', a:'Primary succession begins in an area where no living organisms ever existed — bare rock, a newly cooled lava flow, a newly created pond. Pioneer species such as lichens must first weather the rock and create soil, so it takes thousands of years. Secondary succession begins in an area that once supported a community but was cleared by fire, flood or human activity; soil is already present along with seeds and nutrients. It is therefore much faster. In both, the community changes through a sequence of seral stages until a stable climax community in equilibrium with the environment is established.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Describe the steps in decomposition.', a:'Fragmentation — detritivores such as earthworms break the detritus into smaller particles. Leaching — water-soluble nutrients percolate into the soil and are precipitated as unavailable salts. Catabolism — bacterial and fungal enzymes degrade the detritus into simpler inorganic substances. Humification — accumulation of dark coloured amorphous humus, which is highly resistant to microbial action and acts as a nutrient reservoir. Mineralisation — humus is further degraded by some microbes, releasing inorganic nutrients into the soil.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b12-12', unit:5, name:'Biodiversity and Conservation', marks:'Unit 5 — Ecology (15)',
    topics:['Concept of biodiversity; patterns of biodiversity','Importance of biodiversity; loss of biodiversity and its causes','Biodiversity conservation: in situ and ex situ','Hotspots, endangered organisms, extinction; Red Data Book','Biosphere reserves, national parks, sanctuaries and Ramsar sites'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Levels of biodiversity', d:'Genetic diversity (variation within a species — Rauwolfia across the Himalayas), species diversity (variety of species in a region), and ecological diversity (variety of ecosystems).'},
      {t:'Latitudinal gradient', d:'Species diversity generally decreases from the equator towards the poles. Reasons: tropical regions have been undisturbed for millions of years allowing long evolutionary time, they have a constant environment promoting niche specialisation, and they receive more solar energy giving higher productivity.'},
      {t:'The evil quartet', d:'Four major causes of biodiversity loss: habitat loss and fragmentation (the most important), over-exploitation, alien species invasion, and co-extinction.'},
      {t:'In situ vs ex situ', d:'In situ conserves the organism in its natural habitat — biosphere reserves, national parks, sanctuaries, sacred groves. Ex situ conserves it outside — zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, cryopreservation.'},
      {t:'Hotspots', d:'Regions with exceptionally high species richness and a high degree of endemism that are under threat. India has the Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, the Indo-Burma region, and the Himalaya.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'S = CA^Z (species–area relationship) ; log S = log C + Z log A', m:'Z is 0.1–0.2 for small areas, 0.6–1.2 for very large areas'},
      {f:'India: 2.4% of world land area, 8.1% of global species diversity', m:'A megadiversity country'},
      {f:'Alien species examples: water hyacinth, Lantana, Parthenium, African catfish (Clarias gariepinus), Nile perch', m:'Invasive species'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing in situ and ex situ methods.','Naming the Amazon as an Indian hotspot.','Forgetting that habitat loss is the single largest cause of biodiversity loss.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the species–area relationship. What is the value of the regression coefficient Z?', a:'Alexander von Humboldt observed that within a region, species richness increases with the area explored, but only up to a limit. The relationship is a rectangular hyperbola described by S = CA^Z, or on a log scale log S = log C + Z log A, where S is species richness, A is area, C is the y-intercept and Z is the slope or regression coefficient. For smaller areas — a continent, a state — Z lies between 0.1 and 0.2 regardless of the taxonomic group. For very large areas such as entire continents, the slope is much steeper, with Z between 0.6 and 1.2.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe the four major causes of biodiversity loss ("the evil quartet").', a:'(i) Habitat loss and fragmentation — the most important cause; clearing of tropical rainforests, destruction of wetlands and fragmentation of large habitats into small patches that cannot sustain populations requiring large territories. (ii) Over-exploitation — hunting and harvesting beyond replacement, which drove the Steller\'s sea cow and the passenger pigeon to extinction and is now depleting marine fisheries. (iii) Alien species invasion — introduced species like water hyacinth, Lantana, Parthenium and the African catfish outcompete and eliminate native species. (iv) Co-extinctions — when a host species becomes extinct, the parasites and mutualists exclusively dependent on it also disappear.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Distinguish between in situ and ex situ conservation, with two examples of each.', a:'In situ conservation protects species in their own natural habitat, so the entire ecosystem and its evolutionary processes are conserved. Examples: biosphere reserves such as Nilgiri and Nanda Devi, national parks such as Corbett and Kaziranga, wildlife sanctuaries, and sacred groves in Khasi and Jaintia hills. Ex situ conservation takes threatened animals and plants out of their habitat and places them in special care. Examples: zoological parks and botanical gardens, seed banks and gene banks, and cryopreservation of gametes and in vitro fertilisation for animals that no longer breed in the wild.'},
      {marks:2, q:'Why is species diversity greatest in the tropics?', a:'Three main reasons. First, temperate regions were repeatedly glaciated while tropical latitudes remained relatively undisturbed for millions of years, giving species a long uninterrupted period to diversify. Second, tropical environments are less seasonal and more constant, which promotes niche specialisation and hence more species. Third, the tropics receive more solar energy, giving higher productivity, which supports greater diversity.'}
    ]}
  ]},
  11:{ groups:[], chapters:[
  { id:'b11-1', unit:1, name:'Diversity of Living Organisms', marks:'—',
    topics:['What is living? Biodiversity; need for classification','Three domains of life; taxonomy and systematics; concept of species','Taxonomical hierarchy; binomial nomenclature','Taxonomical aids: herbarium, botanical gardens, museum, zoological parks, keys','Five kingdom classification; Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia','Viruses, viroids, lichens','Plant kingdom: algae, bryophytes, pteridophytes, gymnosperms, angiosperms','Animal kingdom: salient features and classification up to phylum/class'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Binomial nomenclature', d:'Given by Carolus Linnaeus. Each name has two parts — generic and specific epithet. Written in Latin, italicised in print, underlined separately when handwritten; genus begins with a capital, species with a small letter.'},
      {t:'Five kingdom system', d:'Proposed by R. H. Whittaker (1969) on the basis of cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.'},
      {t:'Lichen', d:'A symbiotic association between an alga (phycobiont, provides food) and a fungus (mycobiont, provides shelter, water and minerals). Excellent pollution indicators — they do not grow in polluted areas.'},
      {t:'Alternation of generations', d:'In bryophytes the dominant phase is the gametophyte (haploid); in pteridophytes and higher plants the dominant phase is the sporophyte (diploid).'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Hierarchy: Kingdom → Phylum/Division → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species', m:'Taxonomic categories'},
      {f:'Monera — prokaryotic ; Protista — unicellular eukaryotic ; Fungi — heterotrophic, chitin wall', m:'Whittaker\'s kingdoms'},
      {f:'Algae: Chlorophyceae (green), Phaeophyceae (brown), Rhodophyceae (red)', m:'Based on pigments'},
      {f:'Fungi: Phycomycetes, Ascomycetes (sac fungi), Basidiomycetes (club fungi), Deuteromycetes (imperfect fungi)', m:'Based on spore type'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Capitalising the species epithet.','Calling viruses living — they are acellular and obligate parasites.','Confusing which phase is dominant in bryophytes and pteridophytes.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'What are the rules of binomial nomenclature?', a:'(i) Names are in Latin and printed in italics; when handwritten, the genus and species are underlined separately. (ii) The first word is the generic name and begins with a capital letter; the second is the specific epithet and begins with a small letter. (iii) The name of the author who described the species is written in abbreviated form after the specific epithet, e.g. Mangifera indica Linn.'},
      {marks:3, q:'On what basis did Whittaker classify organisms into five kingdoms?', a:'He used five criteria: cell structure (prokaryotic or eukaryotic), thallus organisation (unicellular or multicellular), mode of nutrition (autotrophic or heterotrophic — and within heterotrophy, saprophytic or holozoic), reproduction, and phylogenetic relationships. This allowed prokaryotes to be separated into Monera, unicellular eukaryotes into Protista, and the heterotrophic cell-wall-bearing fungi to be taken out of the plant kingdom into Fungi.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b11-2', unit:2, name:'Structural Organisation in Plants and Animals', marks:'—',
    topics:['Morphology of flowering plants: root, stem, leaf, inflorescence, flower, fruit and seed','Description of families: Solanaceae, Fabaceae, Liliaceae','Anatomy of flowering plants: tissues and tissue systems; anatomy of dicot and monocot root, stem and leaf','Secondary growth','Animal tissues: epithelial, connective, muscular and neural','Morphology and anatomy of the cockroach (or frog)'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Meristematic vs permanent tissue', d:'Meristems divide actively (apical, intercalary, lateral). Permanent tissues have lost the power of division — simple (parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma) and complex (xylem, phloem).'},
      {t:'Dicot vs monocot root', d:'Dicot root: 2–4 xylem bundles (tetrarch), cambium appears later, secondary growth occurs. Monocot root: many xylem bundles (polyarch), large pith, no secondary growth.'},
      {t:'Secondary growth', d:'Increase in girth due to the vascular cambium and cork cambium. Produces annual rings, which allow the age of a tree to be estimated (dendrochronology).'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Floral formula symbols: Br (bract), ⊕ actinomorphic, % zygomorphic, K calyx, C corolla, A androecium, G gynoecium', m:'Family descriptions'},
      {f:'Epithelial tissue types: squamous, cuboidal, columnar, ciliated, glandular, compound', m:'Animal tissues'},
      {f:'Cockroach: 10 abdominal segments, mouth parts biting and chewing, hepatic caeca, malpighian tubules for excretion', m:'Periplaneta americana'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Confusing tetrarch/polyarch between dicot and monocot roots.','Forgetting that monocots show no secondary growth.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Give three anatomical differences between a dicot stem and a monocot stem.', a:'(i) Vascular bundles in a dicot stem are arranged in a ring, conjoint, collateral and open (with cambium); in a monocot stem they are scattered in the ground tissue, conjoint, collateral and closed. (ii) A dicot stem has distinct cortex, endodermis, pericycle and pith; a monocot stem has undifferentiated ground tissue. (iii) Secondary growth occurs in a dicot stem due to the cambium but not in a monocot stem. A monocot bundle is also surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath and often has a lysigenous water cavity.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b11-3', unit:3, name:'Cell: Structure and Function', marks:'—',
    topics:['Cell theory; prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells','Cell envelope; cell membrane; cell wall','Cell organelles: endomembrane system, mitochondria, plastids, ribosomes, cytoskeleton, cilia, flagella, centrioles','Nucleus: nuclear membrane, chromatin, nucleolus','Biomolecules: structure and function of proteins, carbohydrates, lipids and nucleic acids','Enzymes: properties, classification, mechanism of action, factors affecting activity','Cell cycle and cell division: mitosis, meiosis and their significance'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Fluid mosaic model', d:'Proposed by Singer and Nicolson (1972). The membrane is a lipid bilayer in which proteins float like icebergs — quasi-fluid, allowing lateral movement, which is essential for growth, endocytosis and cell division.'},
      {t:'Semi-autonomous organelles', d:'Mitochondria and plastids have their own circular DNA, 70S ribosomes, and divide by fission — evidence for the endosymbiotic theory.'},
      {t:'Enzyme action', d:'The substrate binds the active site forming an enzyme–substrate complex; the enzyme lowers the activation energy, the reaction proceeds, and the product is released. Affected by temperature, pH, substrate concentration and inhibitors.'},
      {t:'Significance of meiosis', d:'Halves the chromosome number so that fertilisation restores it, and produces variation through crossing over and independent assortment — the basis of evolution.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Cell cycle: G₁ → S → G₂ → M ; interphase is about 95% of the cycle', m:'DNA replicates in S phase'},
      {f:'Mitosis: PMAT — prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase ; equational division', m:'2n → 2n'},
      {f:'Meiosis I prophase: leptotene → zygotene → pachytene (crossing over) → diplotene (chiasmata) → diakinesis', m:'Reductional division'},
      {f:'Prokaryotic ribosome 70S (50S + 30S) ; Eukaryotic 80S (60S + 40S)', m:'Ribosomes'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying crossing over occurs in zygotene — it occurs in pachytene.','Forgetting that DNA replicates once (in S phase) but the cell divides twice in meiosis.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane.', a:'Proposed by Singer and Nicolson in 1972. The membrane consists of a bilayer of phospholipids with their polar heads outward and non-polar tails inward, giving a self-sealing barrier. Protein molecules are embedded in this bilayer — peripheral proteins on the surface and integral proteins partly or wholly buried in it — like a mosaic of icebergs floating in a sea of lipid. The membrane is quasi-fluid, so lipids and proteins can move laterally within the bilayer. This fluidity is essential for cell growth, formation of intercellular junctions, secretion, endocytosis and cell division.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Give three differences between mitosis and meiosis.', a:'(i) Mitosis involves one division producing two daughter cells; meiosis involves two successive divisions producing four. (ii) In mitosis the chromosome number remains the same (equational); in meiosis it is halved (reductional). (iii) Mitosis produces genetically identical cells; meiosis produces genetically varied cells because of crossing over in pachytene and independent assortment at metaphase I. Mitosis occurs in somatic cells for growth and repair; meiosis occurs in reproductive cells for gamete formation.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b11-4', unit:4, name:'Plant Physiology', marks:'—',
    topics:['Transport in plants: diffusion, osmosis, plasmolysis, imbibition; water potential','Absorption and transport of water and minerals; transpiration; translocation','Mineral nutrition: essential elements, macro and micronutrients, deficiency symptoms; nitrogen metabolism','Photosynthesis: site, pigments, light and dark reactions, C3 and C4 pathways, photorespiration, factors affecting','Respiration: glycolysis, Krebs cycle, electron transport system, fermentation, respiratory quotient','Plant growth and development: phases, growth regulators, photoperiodism, vernalisation'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Water potential', d:'Ψ_w = Ψ_s + Ψ_p. Pure water has zero water potential; adding solute makes it negative. Water always moves from higher to lower water potential.'},
      {t:'Transpiration pull', d:'The most accepted theory of ascent of sap. Transpiration from the leaves creates a negative pressure that pulls a continuous column of water up the xylem, held together by cohesion and adhesion.'},
      {t:'C4 pathway', d:'In plants like maize and sugarcane, CO₂ is first fixed in mesophyll cells as a 4-carbon acid (OAA) by PEP carboxylase, then transported to bundle sheath cells where it is released for the Calvin cycle. This concentrates CO₂ and avoids photorespiration, making C4 plants more efficient in hot dry climates.'},
      {t:'Photorespiration', d:'RuBisCO binds O₂ instead of CO₂ at high O₂ and low CO₂, producing phosphoglycolate. There is no synthesis of sugar, ATP or NADPH — it is a wasteful process, absent in C4 plants.'},
      {t:'Respiratory quotient', d:'RQ = CO₂ evolved / O₂ consumed. Carbohydrates give RQ = 1, fats about 0.7, proteins about 0.9, and organic acids more than 1.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Ψ_w = Ψ_s + Ψ_p', m:'Water potential'},
      {f:'6CO₂ + 12H₂O →(light, chlorophyll) C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ + 6H₂O', m:'Photosynthesis'},
      {f:'Glycolysis: glucose → 2 pyruvate + 2 ATP + 2 NADH (cytoplasm)', m:'EMP pathway'},
      {f:'Aerobic respiration of one glucose = 38 ATP (36 in eukaryotes)', m:'Net gain'},
      {f:'Auxin (apical dominance) · Gibberellin (bolting) · Cytokinin (cell division) · ABA (stress hormone) · Ethylene (ripening)', m:'Plant growth regulators'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying the light reaction happens only in daylight and the dark reaction only at night — the "dark" reaction simply does not need light directly.','Confusing the number of ATP from glycolysis (net 2) with total respiration (38).','Forgetting that photolysis of water occurs in PS II, not PS I.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:5, q:'Compare C3 and C4 pathways of photosynthesis.', a:'C3 pathway (Calvin cycle): the primary CO₂ acceptor is RuBP, a 5-carbon compound; the first stable product is 3-phosphoglyceric acid, a 3-carbon compound; the enzyme is RuBisCO; it occurs only in mesophyll cells; there is no Kranz anatomy; photorespiration occurs and the optimum temperature is lower. C4 pathway (Hatch–Slack): the primary acceptor is PEP, a 3-carbon compound in the mesophyll; the first stable product is oxaloacetic acid, a 4-carbon compound; the enzyme is PEP carboxylase which has a much higher affinity for CO₂ and no affinity for O₂; the leaf shows Kranz anatomy with large bundle sheath cells where the Calvin cycle occurs; photorespiration is absent; C4 plants such as maize, sugarcane and sorghum tolerate higher temperatures and show greater productivity.'},
      {marks:3, q:'What is photorespiration? Why is it considered wasteful?', a:'RuBisCO has affinity for both CO₂ and O₂. When the O₂ concentration is high and CO₂ low, RuBP binds O₂ instead, forming one molecule of phosphoglycerate and one of phosphoglycolate. The glycolate is metabolised through the peroxisome and mitochondrion, releasing CO₂. It is wasteful because no sugar is synthesised, no ATP is produced, and NADPH is actually consumed — carbon already fixed is lost. It is absent in C4 plants because their Kranz anatomy keeps the CO₂ concentration around RuBisCO high.'}
    ]},
  { id:'b11-5', unit:5, name:'Human Physiology', marks:'—',
    topics:['Digestion and absorption: alimentary canal, digestive glands, enzymes, disorders','Breathing and exchange of gases: respiratory organs, mechanism, transport of gases, regulation','Body fluids and circulation: blood composition, blood groups, coagulation, heart, cardiac cycle, ECG, double circulation','Excretory products and their elimination: nephron, urine formation, counter current mechanism, regulation','Locomotion and movement: types of movement, muscle contraction, skeletal system, joints and disorders','Neural control and coordination: neuron, nerve impulse, central and peripheral nervous system, reflex action, sense organs','Chemical coordination and integration: endocrine glands and hormones, mechanism of hormone action'],
    concepts:[
      {t:'Cardiac cycle', d:'One complete heartbeat lasting 0.8 s: atrial systole 0.1 s, ventricular systole 0.3 s, joint diastole 0.4 s. Stroke volume ≈ 70 mL, cardiac output ≈ 5 L/min.'},
      {t:'Oxygen dissociation curve', d:'Sigmoid. Shifts right (less affinity, more unloading) with high CO₂, high H⁺, low pH, high temperature — the Bohr effect, which favours release of O₂ in active tissues.'},
      {t:'Counter current mechanism', d:'The loop of Henle and the vasa recta run in opposite directions, maintaining an increasing osmolarity gradient from cortex (300 mOsm/L) to inner medulla (1200 mOsm/L), which allows concentrated urine to be produced.'},
      {t:'Sliding filament theory', d:'Muscle contraction occurs when the thin actin filaments slide over the thick myosin filaments. Ca²⁺ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum exposes the binding sites on actin; myosin heads form cross bridges and pull, using ATP. The A band stays constant while the I band and H zone shorten.'},
      {t:'Nerve impulse', d:'Resting potential is about −70 mV (Na⁺ outside, K⁺ inside, maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump). A stimulus opens Na⁺ channels causing depolarisation to +30 mV — the action potential — which propagates along the axon.'}
    ],
    formulas:[
      {f:'Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate ≈ 70 × 72 ≈ 5000 mL/min', m:'Circulation'},
      {f:'Tidal volume 500 mL · IRV 2500–3000 · ERV 1000–1100 · RV 1100–1200 · Vital capacity ≈ 3500–4500 mL', m:'Respiratory volumes'},
      {f:'GFR ≈ 125 mL/min ≈ 180 L/day ; urine ≈ 1.5 L/day (99% reabsorbed)', m:'Kidney'},
      {f:'Enzymes: salivary amylase (starch) · pepsin (protein) · trypsin, lipase, amylase (pancreatic) · maltase, lactase, sucrase (intestinal)', m:'Digestion'},
      {f:'Hormones: insulin ↓ glucose · glucagon ↑ glucose · thyroxine (BMR) · ADH (water reabsorption) · aldosterone (Na⁺)', m:'Endocrine'}
    ],
    mistakes:['Saying pepsin works in the intestine — it needs the acidic pH of the stomach.','Confusing vital capacity with total lung capacity (which includes the residual volume).','Forgetting that the A band does not change length during muscle contraction.'],
    questions:[
      {marks:5, q:'Describe the mechanism of urine formation.', a:'It occurs in three steps. (i) Glomerular filtration — blood is filtered under high pressure through the three-layered filtration membrane of the glomerulus and Bowman\'s capsule; everything except proteins and blood cells passes into the capsule. The glomerular filtration rate is about 125 mL/min, or 180 litres a day. (ii) Reabsorption — about 99% of the filtrate is reabsorbed. Glucose, amino acids, Na⁺ and water are reabsorbed actively or passively, chiefly in the proximal convoluted tubule; the descending limb of Henle\'s loop is permeable to water but not salts, while the ascending limb is the reverse. (iii) Secretion — H⁺, K⁺ and ammonia are secreted into the tubule, chiefly in the distal convoluted tubule, to maintain the ionic and acid–base balance. The counter current mechanism of the loop of Henle and vasa recta maintains a concentration gradient in the medulla, enabling the formation of concentrated urine; ADH increases the water permeability of the collecting duct.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Explain the sliding filament theory of muscle contraction.', a:'A nerve impulse at the neuromuscular junction releases acetylcholine, which generates an action potential in the sarcolemma. This causes the sarcoplasmic reticulum to release Ca²⁺. Calcium binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin and exposing the myosin binding sites on the actin filament. The energised myosin head binds actin, forming a cross bridge, and then pivots, pulling the thin filament towards the centre of the sarcomere — this is the power stroke, and ADP and Pi are released. A fresh ATP binds the myosin head, releasing it from actin, and the cycle repeats. The Z lines are drawn closer, the I band and H zone shorten, but the A band remains the same length. Relaxation follows when Ca²⁺ is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum.'},
      {marks:3, q:'Describe the cardiac cycle.', a:'One cardiac cycle takes 0.8 seconds at 72 beats per minute. It begins with joint diastole, when all chambers are relaxed and blood flows from the atria into the ventricles through the open tricuspid and bicuspid valves. The SA node then fires and atrial systole occurs (0.1 s), pushing about 30% more blood into the ventricles. Ventricular systole follows (0.3 s): the ventricles contract, the AV valves close producing the first heart sound "lubb", pressure rises, the semilunar valves open and blood is ejected into the aorta and pulmonary artery. Then comes ventricular diastole (0.5 s): the semilunar valves close producing the second sound "dupp", and as ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure the AV valves open again. Stroke volume is about 70 mL, giving a cardiac output of roughly 5 litres per minute.'}
    ]}
  ]}
  }
};
